===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|honble}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}
* {{l|en|Honorable}} {{q|honorific}}
* {{q|British spelling:}} {{l|en|honourable}}, {{l|en|Honourable}} {{q|honorific}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|honourable}}, from {{der|en|fro|honorable}}, {{m|fro|honurable}}, from {{der|en|la|honōrābilis}}, from {{m|la|honōrō||I honour}}; cognate with {{cog|it|onorabile}}, {{cog|es|honorable}}. [[surface analysis|Surface analysis]] is {{af|en|honor|-able}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{enPR|ŏnʹərəbl|ŏnʹrəbl}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈɒnəɹəbl̩/|/ˈɒnɹəbl̩/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{enPR|ŏnʹərəbl|ŏnʹrəbl}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈɑnəɹəbl̩/|/ˈɑnɹəbl̩/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-honorable.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyph|en|hon|or|able}}, {{hyph|en|honor|able|nocaption=1}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}} {{tlb|en|American spelling}}

# [[worthy|Worthy]] of [[respect]]; [[respectable]].
# {{lb|en|politics}} A courtesy title, given in Britain and the Commonwealth to a [[cabinet minister]], [[minister of state]], or [[senator]], and in the United States to the president, vice president, congresspeople, state governors and legislators, and mayors.

====Usage notes====
* Historically used in the United States (early 1900s) in mockery of Japanese people (or {{m|en|'''honorable''' [[Jap]]s}}). [[Category:American English]] [[Category:English ethnic slurs]]

